From ec58769cd70cf4872873073f2f91807a535c125c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 22:28:48 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Open a folder dropped on the threads sidebar as a project of
 its own

The threads sidebar lists one group per project and already has a way to add
one -- the "Add Project" popover, which ends in MultiWorkspace::open_project.
It accepted no drop at all: a folder dragged from a file manager onto the
list fell through and nothing happened. This wires ExternalPaths onto the
sidebar root and sends each dropped folder down that same open_project path,
so a drop does exactly what picking the folder in the popover does, restored
session included.

Three decisions worth stating.

One project per folder. Dropping three folders adds three groups, not one
multi-root project. open_project given several paths would build the
multi-root one; the loop calls it once per folder instead, sequentially, so
the empty-workspace replacement open_project performs runs once and is not
raced by the next folder.

A folder that is already a project anywhere is skipped, not re-activated.
open_project on its own would find an existing workspace in THIS window and
activate it, which moves focus -- the wrong answer to a drop on a list -- and
it looks at no other window, so a folder open elsewhere would be opened a
second time here. is_open_as_project walks every MultiWorkspace window and
matches both a listed group key (a group with no open workspace still counts)
and an open workspace rooted at exactly that folder. It runs right before each
open, not once up front, so the same folder dropped twice opens once. A folder
nested inside an open project is not that project and still opens.

Files are skipped. Unlike 0018's editor-area drop, where a file has an obvious
meaning, a file dropped on a list of projects has none, so this needs no
setting: dropping a folder on the sidebar had no behaviour before, and the one
it gains is the only reading the gesture has.

The drag highlight is drop_target_background blended over the sidebar's own
background, not set in its place. That theme colour is translucent -- it is
meant to sit on top of something, the way pane.rs paints it on an overlay
div -- and as the sidebar's own bg it replaced the opaque fill, so the window
behind showed through for as long as the drag lasted.

"Another instance" means another window of this process. Zed is
single-instance per release channel -- a second launch hands its paths to the
first over a socket -- so every window a user sees is reachable from
cx.windows(). A Zeo process or a ZED_STATELESS one is a separate process with
its own database, and is not consulted.

The drop test covers the cross-window case on purpose: removing the
is_open_as_project check still passes a same-window-only test, because
open_project dedupes within its own window. Measured by mutation both ways.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
